  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     V4 HERO OVERRIDES
     ─────────────────
     v4 inherits everything v3 does and changes ONE thing: the
     hero photo now spans the full viewport, and the navtab strip
     that v3 places in a separate dark band BELOW the photo is
     instead floated as an absolute overlay across the bottom of
     the photo. End result:

       v3:  [ hero photo ] / [ dark band w/ navtab ]   = 100vh
       v4:  [ hero photo with navtab glassed on bottom ]  = 100vh

     Companion JS (top of file) appends the band INTO the hero
     when svc-v4-active is set, so position:absolute resolves
     against the hero rather than the document.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* Hero spans the full viewport. Text cluster anchored to
     the TOP-LEFT (magazine-cover layout) — V2's hero used
     flex-end + bottom-padding to anchor content to the
     bottom of a content-height box. With the photo now full
     viewport, top-left is the right composition: the eyebrow
     leads the eye in from the top, the headline gets the
     space it deserves, and the photo's bottom-right area
     stays uncluttered for the floating navtab below. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero {
    min-height: 100vh !important;
    position: relative !important;
    justify-content: flex-start !important;
    padding-top: clamp(96px, 12vh, 152px) !important;
    padding-bottom: 0 !important;
  }

  /* Band is now a transparent positioning frame — the photo
     extends through to the bottom edge unobstructed. The
     strip's own drop-shadow is what carries the "floating"
     read; a band gradient was masking that shadow against
     dark pixels and making it invisible. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions {
    position: absolute !important;
    left: 0 !important;
    right: 0 !important;
    bottom: 0 !important;
    height: auto !important;
    min-height: 0 !important;
    z-index: 5 !important;
    padding-bottom: clamp(64px, 9vh, 104px) !important;
    background: transparent !important;
  }

  /* Drop the v3 green hairline divider — there's no longer a
     hard photo→band seam to mark. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions::before {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* ─── Floating drop-shadow stack — three layers to build
         real perceived altitude:

         1. Contact shadow (5px / 12px blur) — close to the
            card, gives it weight where it "presses" toward
            the surface. Without this the card looks
            disconnected from the scene.
         2. Lift shadow (24px / 56px blur) — defines the
            visible drop. This is the layer the eye reads
            as "the card is floating".
         3. Ambient pool (48px / 96px blur, large negative
            spread) — wide diffuse halo that suggests the
            light source overhead. Negative spread keeps the
            pool from leaking far past the card's footprint
            so the photo around the card stays untouched.
         4. Inset top highlight — catches ambient light from
            above, reinforces the 3D feel.
       ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip {
    box-shadow:
      /* Contact shadow — tight to the card edge, defines
         the card's footprint. Slight horizontal offset
         consistent with a top-front overhead light source. */
      2px 10px 18px -2px rgba(4, 12, 20, 0.55),
      /* Primary lift — large drop for visible altitude.
         The 40px y-offset is what reads as "this card is
         clearly hovering above the surface". */
      3px 42px 84px -10px rgba(4, 12, 20, 0.78),
      /* Atmospheric pool — wide diffuse halo that anchors
         the card visually. Negative spread keeps it from
         leaking far past the card's footprint, preserving
         the photo around the edges. */
      0 88px 140px -36px rgba(4, 12, 20, 0.62),
      /* Top edge highlight — catches overhead ambient light,
         reinforces the dimensional read. */
      0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12) inset !important;
  }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     V4 FINAL CTA OVERRIDES
     ──────────────────────
     Replaces v3's solid light-band backdrop with a photo
     ground (garage shot, chargers visible) + a refined scrim
     stack for legibility. The .svc-final-card becomes a
     translucent glass slab so the photo behind it reads
     through, with backdrop-filter blur softening whatever
     pixels are immediately behind the copy.

     Inspiration aligned with fleets-v2 + highway-v3 final
     CTAs (photo backdrop + multi-stop scrim) but tuned to
     multifamily's lighter editorial register — the glass
     card replaces the photo-only "centered text" treatment
     so the page still has visible structure, not just text
     floating over a photo.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  /* Replace v3's solid light fill with the garage photo +
     a multi-stop scrim, COMPOSED AROUND the logos.

     The photo has Evium-branded charger logos at image
     y=31-35%, x=37-63%. With default `center` cover
     positioning they project to section y=236-313, x=533-893
     — exactly where centered text would sit, covering the
     branding.

     Two adjustments to keep the logos visible:
       1. object-position: 50% 100% — anchors the BOTTOM of
          the image to the bottom of the section, which
          shifts the logos UP in the visible section to
          roughly y=185-272 (upper third). The upper portion
          of the section now reads as "two branded chargers
          framed by parked vehicles."
       2. The radial-darken spotlight is moved DOWN to 50% 70%
          and tightened so it ONLY covers the text area in
          the lower half — leaves the upper half (where the
          logos sit) at the lighter scrim intensity so the
          branding is fully readable.

     Three layered backgrounds:
       1. Photo (bottom layer = back).
       2. Vertical anchor scrim — heavier on top + bottom
          to seam into the dark page; lets the upper-middle
          breathe at ~25% darkness so logos read clearly.
       3. Centered text-spotlight in the LOWER half (50% 70%)
          where the title + subhead + CTAs live. */
  /* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Logo positions in the source image — kept as a note
     for future iterations even though we're not composing
     around them in this version (the badge resolution
     in the source isn't tight enough to make them a focal
     point worth protecting):
       Image: 1537 × 1023
       Left charger EVIUM:   x=37–42%,  y=31–33% of image
       Right charger EVIUM:  x=55–62%,  y=33–35% of image
     At cover-fit on standard desktop viewports both badges
     project to the section's center-middle band, behind
     the centered text — they read as part of the photo's
     atmosphere rather than as branded callouts.
     ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /* Section background — photo + scrim composition tuned
     for centered editorial copy. Three layers, painted
     bottom-to-top in the rule:

       1. Photo — full-bleed garage shot, centered cover.
       2. Vertical anchor scrim — heavy at top + bottom
          edges to seam into the dark page above/below;
          transparent in the middle so the photo's natural
          lighting + warm-cool palette reads through.
       3. Centered radial pool — soft elliptical darken at
          50% / 52% to lift contrast under the headline +
          subhead + CTAs without darkening the photo's
          edges where the parked vehicles frame the shot.
   */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final::before {
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        ellipse 62% 52% at 50% 52%,
        rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.58) 0%,
        rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.22) 58%,
        transparent 85%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        180deg,
        rgba(10, 24, 34, 0.72) 0%,
        rgba(10, 24, 34, 0.22) 16%,
        rgba(10, 24, 34, 0.22) 84%,
        rgba(10, 24, 34, 0.72) 100%
      ),
      url('/images/heroes/multifamily-cta-garage.png') center / cover no-repeat !important;
  }

  /* Section padding — generous, editorial. Reset from any
     prior asymmetric overrides; centered composition gets
     symmetric breathing room top + bottom. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final {
    isolation: isolate !important;
    padding-top: clamp(112px, 14vh, 168px) !important;
    padding-bottom: clamp(112px, 14vh, 168px) !important;
  }

  /* Centered brand-green ambient halo behind the copy —
     adds depth without competing with the photo. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-glow {
    background: radial-gradient(
      ellipse at 50% 52%,
      rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.22) 0%,
      rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.07) 42%,
      transparent 72%
    ) !important;
    filter: blur(110px) !important;
  }

  /* Strip the white card chrome — copy sits directly on
     the scrimmed photo. Centered with editorial max-width. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-card {
    background: none !important;
    border: 0 !important;
    box-shadow: none !important;
    backdrop-filter: none !important;
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: none !important;
    margin-left: auto !important;
    margin-right: auto !important;
    max-width: 780px !important;
    text-align: center !important;
    padding: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px) clamp(24px, 4vw, 56px) !important;
  }

  /* CTA row — re-center after any earlier overrides. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta-row--centered.svc-final-actions {
    justify-content: center !important;
  }

  /* ─── Premium typography on the photo ──────────────────
     Each text element gets a 4-layer shadow stack tuned
     for editorial photo overlays:
       • 1px tight  — character-edge crispness
       • 4px mid    — ambient halo softens the text against
                      mixed-luminance pixels
       • 12px wide  — atmospheric drop
       • 40px deep  — anchors the type to the photo with
                      the depth you'd see in printed
                      magazine overlays
     ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  /* Headline — slightly tightened tracking for premium
     feel; full-strength white. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-title {
    color: #ffffff !important;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em !important;
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.92),
      0 4px 12px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.78),
      0 12px 36px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.60),
      0 40px 80px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.42) !important;
  }

  /* "Electrified." accent word — brand green with a
     two-step ambient brand glow stacked over the dark
     legibility shadows. The 24px green halo is what reads
     as "this word is glowing"; the 60px wider green is
     the atmospheric brand wash. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-title-accent {
    color: #2DC856 !important;
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.92),
      0 0 24px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.50),
      0 0 60px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.30),
      0 8px 32px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.62) !important;
  }

  /* Subhead — quieter white, scaled-down shadow stack. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-sub {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88) !important;
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.92),
      0 2px 14px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.78) !important;
  }

  /* Footnote — quietest, but still photo-legible. */
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-final-footnote {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.58) !important;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.85) !important;
  }

  /* ─── Premium CTA buttons ──────────────────────────────
     Both buttons get refined treatments specifically for
     sitting on a photo:
       • Primary: brand-green button + 3-layer shadow that
         reads as "lit on the photo" — green inner glow,
         green ambient lift, dark atmospheric drop.
       • Ghost: white-bordered glass button with 10px
         backdrop-blur saturation pop. Plays as a confident
         secondary, not an afterthought.
     Both have ease-out hover transitions for a tactile
     feel — buttons lift slightly on hover.
     ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

  html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta--primary.svc-cta--final-btn {
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16) inset,
      0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.42) inset,
      0 4px 16px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.48),
      0 12px 32px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.30),
      0 24px 64px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.45) !important;
    transition: transform 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
                box-shadow 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) !important;
  }
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta--primary.svc-cta--final-btn:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px) !important;
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.22) inset,
      0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.48) inset,
      0 6px 20px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.58),
      0 18px 44px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.38),
      0 32px 72px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.50) !important;
  }

  html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta--ghost.svc-cta--final-btn {
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.34) !important;
    color: #ffffff !important;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06) !important;
    backdrop-filter: blur(10px) saturate(1.18) !important;
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(10px) saturate(1.18) !important;
    text-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(8, 18, 26, 0.55) !important;
    transition: transform 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
                background 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
                border-color 280ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) !important;
  }
  html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta--ghost.svc-cta--final-btn:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px) !important;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.14) !important;
    border-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.58) !important;
  }

  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     V4 MOBILE OVERRIDES
     ──────────────────
     Scoped to BOTH html.svc-v4-active AND a max-width: 760px
     media query, so:
       - Desktop is completely unaffected (the rules don't match
         above 860px viewport — same breakpoint HeaderV4 uses
         to swap to its mobile bar).
       - fleets-v2 and highway-v3 are completely unaffected
         (they don't add the svc-v4-active class).

     Two issues this block fixes:

     1. Navtab strip (.svc-stat-strip) was using
        `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr))`
        from the base template. At ~390px viewport that resolves
        to 2 columns, so the 3 stat tiles wrap as 2-on-top + 1-
        below — the orphan reads as "expecting a 4th item." Force
        a single horizontal row of 3 equal tiles, tighten padding
        and gap, drop the trailing arrow, and stack the icon +
        text vertically inside each tile so the text doesn't run
        out of horizontal room at narrow widths.

     2. Hero image (.svc-hero-bg-img) was using the base
        `object-position: center 30%` which biases toward the top
        of the photo — meant for landscape-oriented desktop crops
        where 30% framing captures the building's roofline + sky
        cleanly. On a portrait phone crop the same offset throws
        the building off-center and shows mostly upper-floor
        balconies + wall. Re-anchoring to `50% 55%` centers the
        building's mid-section (the most architecturally
        photogenic part) in the visible portrait viewport.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    /* ─── Hero box: fit BELOW the sticky mobile navbar AND
       anchor content to the LOWER HALF for magazine-cover
       composition ───

       `calc(100dvh - 80px)` fills the visible area below
       the sticky navbar exactly.

       Switched from `flex-start` (content hugging the top
       under the navbar) to `flex-end` — the eyebrow / title
       / sub / CTAs now sit at the BOTTOM of the hero, just
       above the navtab strip. The mobile hero photo
       (building shot, looking up at balconies) gets the
       top half to itself, making the composition read like
       a magazine cover: photo dominant, headline anchored
       at the lower-left like editorial credit. Combined
       with the strip's padding-bottom (set elsewhere) the
       text cluster + strip together fill the bottom ~50%
       of the hero, leaving the top ~50% as pure photo
       breathing room. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero {
      min-height: calc(100dvh - 80px) !important;
      /* Content top-anchored: text cluster sits HIGH in the
         hero (just below the photo's top breathing room),
         with the CTA row pushed down to the BOTTOM of the
         hero's available content area via margin-top:auto
         (rule below). This creates the three-zone
         composition the user wanted:
           Zone 1: photo + text cluster (eyebrow/title/sub)
                   stacked at the top
           Zone 2: visually pleasing breathing room
           Zone 3: CTAs, then more breathing room, then the
                   floating navtab strip at the bottom. */
      justify-content: flex-start !important;
      padding-top: clamp(72px, 10vh, 110px) !important;
      /* Padding-bottom holds room for the navtab strip
         (~97 tall, overhanging from band's padding-bottom
         ~140) plus a visible ~40-60px gap between CTAs
         and the strip's top edge. */
      padding-bottom: clamp(285px, 36vh, 320px) !important;
    }
    /* Force the hero-content block to fill the hero's
       available space so margin-top:auto on the CTA row
       can push it to the bottom — creating the
       big gap between sub and CTAs the user requested. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero-content {
      display: flex !important;
      flex-direction: column !important;
      flex: 1 1 auto !important;
      min-height: 0 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-cta-row {
      margin-top: auto !important;
    }

    /* ─── Hero text content: compressed to fit alongside
           the bottom-anchored navtab strip ───
       The base clamp values (title 2.2-4.4rem, sub 1-1.18rem,
       CTAs 0.74rem) plus their margins build a heroContent
       block that's ~389px tall at 430px viewport — most of
       the available below-navbar space on a phone with
       chrome visible. Dropping each step by ~15-20% gives
       the photo + navtab room without sacrificing
       readability:
         · Title 1.5-2.1rem caps total title height at ~100px
           (was 150px+).
         · Sub 0.86-0.95rem with tighter line-height shaves
           ~30px off a 5-line paragraph.
         · Margins between blocks compress from clamp(16-26)
           to clamp(8-14) since the content stack is shorter
           overall and doesn't need as much breathing room.
         · CTA padding tightens so buttons stack a bit
           tighter without cramping the touch target. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero-eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: clamp(10px, 1.4vh, 16px) !important;
      font-size: 0.66rem !important;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em !important;
    }
    /* Title bumped up for more impact on the magazine-cover
       composition. Stronger text-shadow so the white type
       reads cleanly against the warm-toned right side of
       the new mobile photo. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero-title {
      margin-bottom: clamp(10px, 1.6vh, 18px) !important;
      font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 7vw, 2.4rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.02 !important;
      letter-spacing: -0.018em !important;
      text-shadow:
        0 2px 16px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55),
        0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero-sub {
      margin-bottom: clamp(16px, 2.2vh, 22px) !important;
      font-size: clamp(0.88rem, 3.4vw, 0.96rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.45 !important;
      text-shadow: 0 1px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45) !important;
    }
    /* CTAs: smaller padding, smaller text, smaller stack
       gap. Stays touch-target-friendly (still 40px+ tall)
       but compresses the row by ~25px. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero .svc-cta-row {
      gap: 10px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero .svc-cta {
      padding: 11px 20px !important;
      font-size: 0.7rem !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero .svc-cta--ghost-on-image {
      padding: 11px 20px !important;
      font-size: 0.7rem !important;
    }

    /* ─── Hero image: mobile-specific shot ───
       The desktop hero is landscape-cropped wide
       (`/images/multifamily-realestate-service-hero.jpg`),
       which doesn't crop well on portrait phones.
       `content: url(...)` on the <img> element swaps the
       rendered image without touching the markup or the
       img's src attribute. Object-fit + sizing CSS still
       apply, so the existing hero-anchor viewport math is
       unchanged — only the actual pixels change. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-hero-bg-img {
      content: url('/images/multifamily-hero-mobile.png') !important;
      object-position: 50% 50% !important;
    }

    /* ─── Navtab: single row, 3 equal columns ─── */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)) !important;
      max-width: 100% !important;
      gap: 1px !important;
      border-radius: 16px !important;
      /* Desktop pulls the strip 40-72px below the hero bottom
         to overhang into the next section's top padding —
         reads as a "lifted card" floating between the photo
         and the next section. On mobile the same negative
         margin made the strip kiss the section divider, with
         no breathing room below it.

         Drop the overhang entirely on mobile: keep the strip
         INSIDE the hero, with the band's increased
         padding-bottom (below) lifting it well above the
         hero's bottom edge so the entry-view composition
         reads as "hero photo + nav floats in the lower
         third" instead of "nav slammed against the divider." */
      margin-bottom: 0 !important;
    }

    /* Tile contents: stack icon over text, drop the trailing
       arrow so all three tiles fit horizontally without
       cramping text. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat--btn {
      flex-direction: column !important;
      align-items: center !important;
      justify-content: center !important;
      gap: 6px !important;
      padding: 12px 6px !important;
      text-align: center !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat-icon {
      width: 28px !important;
      height: 28px !important;
      border-radius: 8px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat-icon svg {
      width: 14px !important;
      height: 14px !important;
    }
    /* Trailing arrow is omitted on mobile — the icon-on-top +
       label-below layout already reads as a tap target, and
       the arrow's horizontal real estate isn't available. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat-arrow {
      display: none !important;
    }
    /* Text stack: tighter gap, value bumps slightly larger so
       it stays the dominant element when the row stops being
       horizontal; label drops a notch in size + tracking so it
       fits a single column without breaking. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat-text {
      gap: 2px !important;
      align-items: center !important;
      text-align: center !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat--btn .svc-stat-value {
      font-size: 0.7rem !important;
      line-height: 1.15 !important;
      font-weight: 800 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions .svc-stat-strip .svc-stat--btn .svc-stat-label {
      font-size: 0.5rem !important;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em !important;
      line-height: 1.2 !important;
    }
    /* Lift the navtab UP from the hero's bottom edge so the
       entry-view composition reads as "hero photo, with the
       nav floating in the lower third" rather than "nav
       slammed at the bottom with a wide empty middle."

       The strip is bottom-anchored, so this padding pushes
       it UP from the hero's bottom edge. A vh-based clamp
       scales the lift with viewport height — more on tall
       phones, less on short ones — keeping the proportion
       roughly consistent as a fraction of the hero box.

       64–96px of padding (≈10% of viewport height) puts the
       strip squarely in the lower third of the hero with
       balanced breathing room above and below it, so the
       photo doesn't feel like dead empty middle space. */
    html.svc-v4-active .svc-v3-hero-actions {
      padding-bottom: clamp(112px, 18vh, 160px) !important;
      padding-left: 16px !important;
      padding-right: 16px !important;
    }

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MOBILE VIEWPORT-ANCHOR FLOW
       ──────────────────────────
       Desktop pageflow:
         · 1st anchor: hero (1 viewport)
         · 2nd anchor: market reality (≈55vh) + top of objections
                       sharing the same viewport, parallax-revealing
                       objections as user continues to scroll
         · 3rd anchor: scope

       Mobile pageflow (this block):
         · 1st anchor: hero (1 viewport)              — set above
         · 2nd anchor: market reality (1 viewport)    — set below
         · 3rd anchor: objections section, sticky head — set below
         · 4th anchor: scope                          — natural flow

       Each anchor still corresponds to the same id (`#service-
       market`, `#service-pain`, `#service-scope`, etc.) so the
       rail nav's targets are unchanged — the only difference is
       that on mobile two of them get their own full viewport
       worth of space instead of sharing one.
       ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

    /* ─── #service-market — full viewport on mobile ───
       Desktop sizes this at 55vh so the lower portion of the
       same viewport shows the pain-section header (the parallax
       co-anchor). On mobile that split doesn't work: a 55dvh
       market section is too short for its own content + the
       pain header would clip awkwardly and the "next section
       peeking under the divider" complaint kicks in.

       Use `calc(100dvh - 80px)` (same calc the hero uses) so the
       market section exactly fills the area below the sticky
       navbar. No peek from the pain section below; the user
       lands here cleanly as the 2nd anchor and scrolls down to
       reach the pain section's top edge (3rd anchor).

       Section content (header + 3 highlight cards) compresses
       to fit the viewport — the cards become a horizontal
       scroll-snap carousel (rules below) so they consume a
       single ~220px row instead of stacking 2-up-then-wrap. */
    /* Section padding compressed hard so the cards row can
       claim its full square real estate below. Section head
       sized tight + small margin-bottom so the 3 stacked
       squares get most of the section's vertical budget. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market {
      min-height: calc(100dvh - 80px) !important;
      padding-top: clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 20px) !important;
      padding-bottom: clamp(16px, 2.4vh, 28px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-section-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 5.4vw, 1.75rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.04 !important;
      margin-bottom: clamp(4px, 0.6vh, 6px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-section-sub {
      font-size: clamp(0.82rem, 2.8vw, 0.9rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.35 !important;
      margin-bottom: 0 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-section-head {
      margin-bottom: clamp(10px, 1.6vh, 16px) !important;
    }
    /* TRUE SQUARE tiles (aspect-ratio: 1) stacked vertically.
       Each card is the same width and height — capped at
       180px so 3 squares + tight header + section padding
       all fit inside calc(100dvh - 80px) on typical mobile
       viewports.

       Math at viewport height 700:
         · section padding 12+16 = 28
         · header (title + sub + margin) ~110
         · 3 cards × 180 + 2 gaps × 8 = 556
         · total = 28 + 110 + 556 = 694 ✓ fits in 700

       At viewport 620 (calc(100dvh - 80px) on 700vh) the
       same math gives 28 + 110 + 556 = 694 — would slightly
       exceed 620, BUT the section's min-height anchors at
       620 and the section grows naturally to fit the content
       (no overflow because no fixed max-height). The hero +
       market combine to ~1320 in absolute scroll, which is
       what we want for a clean 2nd viewport anchor.

       Internal card layout: icon on top, title middle, body
       bottom (vertical content within a square frame —
       matches the desktop tile feel). Body line-clamped to
       2 lines so long copy doesn't overflow the square. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-narrative-grid {
      display: flex !important;
      flex-direction: column !important;
      grid-template-columns: none !important;
      gap: clamp(6px, 1vh, 10px) !important;
      align-items: center !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-narrative-card {
      /* Width capped at ~90vw / 420px. Height grows freely
         with content (no aspect-ratio, no max-height) so the
         full body text always renders and the cards feel
         appropriately tall for readable type. Padding is
         bumped from the previous compact sizing so the text
         has visible breathing room from the card edges. */
      width: clamp(280px, 90vw, 420px) !important;
      aspect-ratio: auto !important;
      padding: clamp(20px, 3vw, 26px) !important;
      display: grid !important;
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr !important;
      grid-template-rows: auto auto !important;
      column-gap: 14px !important;
      row-gap: 12px !important;
      align-items: center !important;
      min-width: 0 !important;
      overflow: visible !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-narrative-icon {
      width: 40px !important;
      height: 40px !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
      grid-row: 1 !important;
      grid-column: 1 !important;
      align-self: center !important;
    }
    /* Title sits on the SAME baseline as the icon. Bumped
       up so the cards feel premium-sized rather than
       compact-utility. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-narrative-title {
      font-size: 1.1rem !important;
      line-height: 1.18 !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
      grid-row: 1 !important;
      grid-column: 2 !important;
      align-self: center !important;
    }
    /* Body bumped to 0.96rem with looser line-height — most
       of the readability gain comes from these two numbers.
       At 90vw card width with ~45 chars per line, body
       reads as 3-4 comfortable lines. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-market .svc-narrative-body {
      font-size: 0.96rem !important;
      line-height: 1.55 !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
      grid-row: 2 !important;
      grid-column: 1 / span 2 !important;
      align-self: start !important;
      display: block !important;
      -webkit-line-clamp: unset !important;
      -webkit-box-orient: unset !important;
      overflow: visible !important;
    }

    /* ─── #service-pain — sticky header + behind-header reveal ───
       Desktop uses a 2-column sticky-left header layout (≥960px
       only). On mobile (≤860px) the section is 1 column and the
       header would just scroll past the user, losing the parallax
       co-anchor that makes the objections reveal feel deliberate.

       Re-engineer the parallax for vertical mobile layout:
         · Header `position: sticky; top: 80px` so it pins right
           under the page navbar while user scrolls through the
           section.
         · Solid dark background on the sticky header so cards
           scrolling past visually go BEHIND it (rather than
           overlapping into a soup of text).
         · Cards in a vertical stack below the header. JS (above)
           uses `headerRect.bottom + 16` as the parallax anchor
           on mobile so cards finish revealing right BEFORE they
           slide behind the header, not while underneath it.
         · Slide direction switched to translateY so cards rise
           up into place (matches the vertical scroll). */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header {
      position: sticky !important;
      top: 80px !important;
      z-index: 3 !important;
      /* Padding stays for internal rhythm. Background is
         handed off to the ::before pseudo (below) which
         spans the FULL viewport width — solid #1A2F3A only
         covering the header's own width left a visible edge
         line on each side where the page-level atmospheric
         green/blue orbs (see .svc-page::before/::after,
         which are fixed-positioned and tint a swath of the
         viewport behind the section) painted a slightly
         different tone OUTSIDE the header vs INSIDE. */
      background: transparent !important;
      padding: clamp(20px, 3vh, 32px) clamp(16px, 4vw, 24px) !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
      box-shadow: none !important;
      overflow: visible !important;
    }
    /* Full-viewport-width opaque slab BEHIND the header text.
       100vw + 50%/translateX centering extends the dark fill
       past the section's content edge to the viewport's
       actual edges, so there's no visible boundary between
       "header bg" and "page bg with atmospheric orb tint"
       on the sides. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header::before {
      content: '' !important;
      position: absolute !important;
      top: 0 !important;
      bottom: 0 !important;
      left: 50% !important;
      transform: translateX(-50%) !important;
      width: 100vw !important;
      background: #1A2F3A !important;
      z-index: -1 !important;
      pointer-events: none !important;
    }
    /* Compact the header's internal type so the sticky band
       doesn't dominate vertical space — it has to leave room
       for cards to show below it on a 700-800dvh viewport. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header .svc-section-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 6vw, 1.9rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.05 !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header .svc-section-sub {
      font-size: clamp(0.82rem, 3.2vw, 0.92rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.45 !important;
      margin-top: clamp(8px, 1.4vh, 12px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header .svc-eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: clamp(6px, 1vh, 10px) !important;
      font-size: 0.6rem !important;
    }
    /* CTA inside the header — tighten to match the compressed
       header type. Stays touch-friendly. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-header .svc-pain-cta {
      margin-top: clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 18px) !important;
      padding: 10px 18px !important;
      font-size: 0.68rem !important;
    }
    /* Card initial state — overrides the desktop translateX
       so the first paint (before JS) matches the mobile
       vertical animation direction. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-card {
      transform: translateY(60px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-card:first-child {
      transform: none !important;
    }
    /* Tighten the section's vertical rhythm so the
       header+cards stack fits with sensible scroll territory
       (each card visible as it enters, header sticks above). */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--pain {
      padding-top: clamp(40px, 5vh, 64px) !important;
      padding-bottom: clamp(64px, 8vh, 96px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-layout {
      gap: clamp(20px, 3vh, 32px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-stack {
      gap: clamp(14px, 2vh, 22px) !important;
    }

    /* ─── Mobile-only pain card tweaks ───
       The text + number size + position overrides moved out
       of this @media block and now apply at ALL widths (see
       the un-wrapped rules earlier in this stylesheet). Two
       MOBILE-SPECIFIC tweaks remain here:

       1. Tighter side padding — desktop has plenty of
          horizontal room so its native clamp(22-30) padding
          looks balanced; mobile cards are narrower and the
          same padding eats too much width, so tighten to a
          flat 18px.
       2. Right-padding on the PROBLEM side — on mobile the
          card stacks vertically (problem on top, solution
          below), so the top-right number now sits inside
          the problem side. Pad the right edge to 56px so
          the number doesn't overlap the problem label/icon.
          On desktop the card is horizontal (problem | arrow
          | solution) and the number sits inside the solution
          side's top-right naturally — no extra padding
          needed there. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-side {
      padding: 18px 18px !important;
      gap: 10px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-pain-side--problem {
      padding-right: 56px !important;
    }

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MOBILE HARDWARE REDESIGN
       ────────────────────────
       Desktop hardware shows 3 cards side-by-side in a wide
       row: EvoCharge | Siemens VersiCharge | GSA-approved.
       The third card has NO image (uses svc-hw-img-fallback)
       because it's an INFORMATIONAL card about hardware
       sourcing standards rather than a specific product.

       On mobile that 3-card row doesn't fit. Reorganize:
         · Row 1: EvoCharge + Siemens (the two ACTUAL charger
           cards) side-by-side, equal columns.
         · Row 2: GSA card spans BOTH columns as an
           informational banner — restyled compact (no image
           stage, just the title + features list as a single
           horizontal note).

       Selector approach: the third card is detected by the
       presence of `.svc-hw-img-fallback` inside it (true
       hardware product cards use `.svc-hw-img` instead).
       Modern :has() makes the distinction without touching
       the markup, so the same template renders both
       desktop and the mobile redesign without forking. */

    /* Override the earlier mobile guard that set hardware
       to min-height: auto. We want hardware viewport-tall
       on mobile so the redesigned layout (2 charger cards +
       GSA banner) has a clear viewport anchor. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware {
      min-height: calc(100dvh - 80px) !important;
      padding-top: clamp(20px, 3vh, 36px) !important;
      padding-bottom: clamp(20px, 3vh, 36px) !important;
      justify-content: center !important;
    }
    /* Section head compressed so the cards row + GSA banner
       claim the rest of the viewport budget. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware .svc-section-head {
      margin-bottom: clamp(14px, 2vh, 22px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware .svc-section-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 5.4vw, 1.75rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.06 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware .svc-section-sub {
      font-size: clamp(0.82rem, 2.8vw, 0.92rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.4 !important;
    }
    /* Force a 2-column grid on the hardware row. The 3rd
       card spans BOTH columns below (info banner mode). */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-grid,
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-grid--3 {
      display: grid !important;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr !important;
      gap: 10px !important;
    }
    /* Card 3 (the one without a real product image, i.e. the
       GSA informational card) spans both columns. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) {
      grid-column: 1 / -1 !important;
    }

    /* TWO PRODUCT CARDS — compressed so they fit in the
       cards row inside calc(100dvh - 80px). */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-stage {
      height: clamp(110px, 16vh, 140px) !important;
      padding: 8px 6px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) {
      padding: 0 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-body {
      padding: 10px 12px 14px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-tag {
      font-size: 0.55rem !important;
      padding: 3px 8px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-name {
      font-size: 0.86rem !important;
      line-height: 1.18 !important;
      margin: 6px 0 4px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-features {
      gap: 4px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-features li {
      font-size: 0.7rem !important;
      line-height: 1.3 !important;
      gap: 6px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:not(:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)) .svc-hw-tick {
      width: 12px !important;
      height: 12px !important;
      flex: 0 0 auto !important;
    }

    /* THIRD CARD — COMPLIANCE STRIP.
       Not a "card" anymore. The third item is not a product;
       it's a compliance / sourcing credential note — the kind
       of content you'd see as a footer-style strip on a spec
       page, NOT as a card with shadow, border, and big padding.

       Strip all card-like chrome: no background, no border, no
       border-radius, no shadow, no large padding. What's left
       is a slim section-footer-style note with:
         · a small uppercase "Compliance & Standards" feel via
           the title styled as an eyebrow tag,
         · the 3 compliance points rendered as inline-flex
           pill-checks (each on its own line on narrow, can
           flow into a row on wider), and
         · a single hairline rule above to visually anchor it
           to the cards-row it sits below. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) {
      /* strip the card chrome */
      background: transparent !important;
      border: none !important;
      border-radius: 0 !important;
      box-shadow: none !important;
      /* slim layout */
      padding: 14px 4px 0 !important;
      margin-top: 4px !important;
      position: relative !important;
      overflow: visible !important;
      transition: none !important;
    }
    /* Hairline divider above to visually attach the strip
       to the cards row above. Brand-green-tinted on the
       left, fading to transparent — feels like an editorial
       separator, not a card outline. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback)::before {
      content: '' !important;
      position: absolute !important;
      top: 0 !important;
      left: 4px !important;
      right: 4px !important;
      height: 1px !important;
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.45) 0%,
        rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.15) 40%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06) 100%
      ) !important;
      transform: none !important;
      display: block !important;
    }
    /* Suppress the desktop ::before scaleX hover effect. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback):hover::before {
      transform: none !important;
    }
    /* No image stage. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-stage {
      display: none !important;
    }
    /* Body sits flush with the strip — no inset padding. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-body {
      padding: 0 !important;
    }
    /* Title becomes a small uppercase EYEBROW tag — the
       "category" of the strip, not a card headline. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-tag {
      display: none !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-name {
      font-size: 0.6rem !important;
      font-weight: 800 !important;
      letter-spacing: 0.16em !important;
      text-transform: uppercase !important;
      line-height: 1 !important;
      margin: 0 0 10px !important;
      color: #2DC856 !important;
    }
    /* Features row: each item is a small check + inline label,
       arranged in a flex-wrap row so the three compliance
       facts read as a horizontal credentials strip rather
       than a vertical bullet list. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-features {
      display: flex !important;
      flex-direction: row !important;
      flex-wrap: wrap !important;
      gap: 6px 14px !important;
      list-style: none !important;
      padding: 0 !important;
      margin: 0 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-features li {
      display: inline-flex !important;
      align-items: center !important;
      gap: 6px !important;
      font-size: 0.72rem !important;
      line-height: 1.3 !important;
      color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.74) !important;
      letter-spacing: 0.01em !important;
    }
    /* Hide the green pill-tick and replace with a subtle
       inline brand mark via ::before so the row reads as
       "credential · credential · credential" rather than
       a list of fat green check buttons. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-tick {
      display: none !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-section--hardware .svc-hw-card:has(.svc-hw-img-fallback) .svc-hw-features li::before {
      content: '' !important;
      display: inline-block !important;
      width: 5px !important;
      height: 5px !important;
      border-radius: 50% !important;
      background: #2DC856 !important;
      flex: 0 0 auto !important;
    }
    /* The hardware "see all" link below the grid also tightens
       so the entire section fits in the viewport. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware .svc-hw-foot {
      margin-top: clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 18px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-hardware .svc-hw-seeall {
      font-size: 0.72rem !important;
      padding: 8px 14px !important;
    }

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FINANCIAL MODELS — mobile tabs + single-card view
       ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Desktop shows all 3 models in a side-by-side grid. On
       mobile that's far too wide and stacking would make the
       section 3× viewport height.

       Mobile redesign:
         · Section anchored to one viewport via min-height
           calc(100dvh - 80px).
         · A tab bar (.svc-v4-fin-tabs, injected by the inline
           JS at the top of the page) sits above the grid with
           one button per model.
         · The grid has data-active="<idx>" set by the tab JS.
           Cards whose index matches data-active are visible;
           all others are display:none. So only one card shows
           at a time — full content of the active model, just
           swapped by tap.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels {
      min-height: calc(100dvh - 80px) !important;
      display: flex !important;
      flex-direction: column !important;
      justify-content: center !important;
      padding-top: clamp(20px, 3vh, 32px) !important;
      padding-bottom: clamp(20px, 3vh, 36px) !important;
    }
    /* Compressed section head — title + sub take less room
       so the active card has more vertical budget. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-section-head {
      margin-bottom: clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 18px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-section-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 5.4vw, 1.85rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.04 !important;
      margin-bottom: 4px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-section-sub {
      font-size: clamp(0.82rem, 2.8vw, 0.92rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.4 !important;
    }

    /* TAB BAR — horizontal pill row with the 3 model names.
       (The default desktop rule below the @media block hides
       this element entirely; this declaration only takes
       effect on mobile.)

       CRITICAL color context: the financial-models section
       sits on a LIGHT band (#f3f7fb feathered into the dark
       page canvas via the ::before pseudo-element). Earlier
       passes styled the tab bar as white-on-translucent-white
       — which disappears against this light section. Inactive
       tabs MUST use dark text on a tinted-dark bg to read
       against #f3f7fb.

       Active tab is the brand-green pill that visually pops
       on either bg; inactive tabs are dark slate-grey on a
       semi-transparent dark slab. */
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-v4-fin-tabs {
      display: flex !important;
      flex-wrap: nowrap !important;
      gap: 4px !important;
      margin: 0 auto clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 18px) !important;
      padding: 4px !important;
      max-width: 100% !important;
      width: 100% !important;
      background: rgba(13, 28, 36, 0.06) !important;
      border: 1px solid rgba(13, 28, 36, 0.10) !important;
      border-radius: 12px !important;
      box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgba(13, 28, 36, 0.06) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-v4-fin-tab {
      flex: 1 1 0 !important;
      min-width: 0 !important;
      padding: 10px 4px !important;
      font-family: inherit !important;
      font-size: 0.78rem !important;
      font-weight: 700 !important;
      letter-spacing: 0 !important;
      line-height: 1.15 !important;
      text-align: center !important;
      /* INACTIVE TABS — dark slate text on the light section
         band so they're clearly readable as interactive
         chips. */
      color: #1d1d1f !important;
      background: transparent !important;
      border: none !important;
      border-radius: 8px !important;
      cursor: pointer !important;
      white-space: nowrap !important;
      overflow: hidden !important;
      text-overflow: ellipsis !important;
      transition:
        color 0.22s ease,
        background 0.22s ease !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-v4-fin-tab[aria-selected="true"] {
      color: #ffffff !important;
      background: #2DC856 !important;
      box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.32),
                  0 6px 14px rgba(45, 200, 86, 0.32) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active .svc-v4-fin-tab:hover:not([aria-selected="true"]) {
      color: #0d1c24 !important;
      background: rgba(13, 28, 36, 0.08) !important;
    }

    /* GRID — single column on mobile. Cards visibility
       controlled by the grid's data-active attribute. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr !important;
      gap: 0 !important;
      max-width: 520px !important;
      margin: 0 auto !important;
    }
    /* Hide every card by default on mobile, then unhide the
       one whose data-fin-card-idx matches data-active. The
       attribute-matching avoids any per-card class toggling
       in JS — the JS just sets a single attribute on the
       grid. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid .svc-fin-card {
      display: none !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid[data-active="0"] .svc-fin-card[data-fin-card-idx="0"],
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid[data-active="1"] .svc-fin-card[data-fin-card-idx="1"],
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid[data-active="2"] .svc-fin-card[data-fin-card-idx="2"],
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-grid[data-active="3"] .svc-fin-card[data-fin-card-idx="3"] {
      display: block !important;
    }
    /* Optional: tighten the active card's internal padding
       to fit the section's viewport budget. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-card {
      padding: 18px 18px 20px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-name {
      font-size: 1.15rem !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-tagline {
      font-size: 0.86rem !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-body {
      font-size: 0.84rem !important;
      line-height: 1.5 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-bullets li {
      font-size: 0.8rem !important;
      line-height: 1.4 !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-financialModels .svc-fin-footnote {
      font-size: 0.76rem !important;
      margin-top: clamp(10px, 1.6vh, 14px) !important;
    }

    /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SOFTWARE — mobile compact stack
       ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Desktop: 2-column grid (left = section head + Owner
       Portal, right = Driver App + Support cards). On mobile
       that 2-col grid auto-stacks to 1-col and the combined
       content (header + portal 6-bullet list + app card + app
       6-bullet list + support card) totals well over 1000px —
       way past the calc(100dvh - 80px) viewport budget.

       Refine on mobile:
         · Section anchored at min-height calc(100dvh - 80px).
         · Section head compressed: small eyebrow + tight title
           + smaller intro paragraph.
         · Owner Portal + Driver App + Support stack in one
           column, each with TIGHTER paddings + smaller type +
           smaller bullet dots — every block shrinks 25-30%.
         · Bullet rows compressed line-height + font so the
           6-item lists feel like a quick scan instead of a
           wall.
       Total target height ≈ 100dvh - 80 so the user lands at
       a clean anchored section; content above the fold tells
       the full software story without scrolling.
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-software {
      min-height: calc(100dvh - 80px) !important;
      padding-top: clamp(16px, 2.4vh, 28px) !important;
      padding-bottom: clamp(20px, 3vh, 32px) !important;
    }
    /* Stack the 2-column grid as one tight column on mobile. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-software .svc-sw-grid {
      display: flex !important;
      flex-direction: column !important;
      gap: clamp(12px, 1.8vh, 18px) !important;
    }
    /* Section head — small eyebrow + tight title + small intro. */
    html.svc-v3-active #service-software .svc-sw-eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.62rem !important;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em !important;
      margin-bottom: 6px !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-software .svc-sw-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 5.6vw, 1.85rem) !important;
      line-height: 1.04 !important;
      margin-bottom: clamp(6px, 1vh, 10px) !important;
    }
    html.svc-v3-active #service-software .svc-sw-intro {
      font-size: 0.82rem !important;
      line-height: 1.4 !important;
      margin-bottom: clamp(12px, 1.6vh, 18px) !important;
    }
